<BaseLayout title="Warehouse &#x26; Distribution Conveyors: Sortation, AS/RS &#x26; Accumulation | PackagingConveyor.com" description="Warehouse and distribution center conveyors: sortation, accumulation, merges, AS/RS integration, and material handling for e-commerce fulfillment." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/industries/warehouse-conveyors/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, articleSchema]}>
<h1>Warehouse &amp; Distribution Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Warehouse and distribution center conveyors move cases, totes, parcels, and pallets through receiving, storage, picking, packing, sortation, and shipping operations. Core conveyor functions include high-speed sortation (routing items to the correct shipping lane), <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/zero-pressure/">zero-pressure accumulation</a> (staging items without contact pressure), merge (combining multiple flows into one line), and AS/RS integration (connecting automated storage with order fulfillment).</p>
<h2 id="sort-heading">Sortation Conveyors</h2>
<p>Sortation conveyors automatically divert products from a main transport line to designated lanes, chutes, or palletizing stations based on barcode scan, RFID read, or dimensioning data. The sorter often sets the facility's maximum order throughput, so its rate and sort accuracy deserve the closest scrutiny in any proposal.</p>
<h3>Sliding Shoe Sorter</h3>
<p>Sliding shoe sorters use rows of small plastic shoes (diverter elements) mounted on a flat conveyor surface. When an item reaches its designated lane, shoes slide laterally across the belt surface, pushing the item off the main line into the lane. Sliding shoe sorters handle a wide range of package sizes and provide gentle, positive diverts with no impact or dropping. Divert angle trades speed for footprint: Hytrol rates its <a href="https://cdn.hytrol.com/ProSort1400_PF.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ProSort 1400 series</a> at up to 650 FPM with a 22-degree divert and up to 350 FPM with a 30-degree divert, for products up to 100 lb.</p>
<h3>Crossbelt Sorter</h3>
<p>Crossbelt sorters use individual carrier cells, each with a short belt conveyor mounted perpendicular to the direction of travel. When a cell reaches the designated sort point, its belt activates, sliding the item off the cell into the lane. Crossbelt sorters handle the widest range of product types: small polybags, envelopes, cartons, and irregularly shaped items. As one example, Interroll rates its <a href="https://www.interroll.com/products/sorters/mx-h-horizontal-crossbelt-sorter" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MX-H horizontal crossbelt sorter</a> at up to 2.7 m/s, 4,000 to 20,000 items per hour, and items up to 50 kg. See <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems</a> for more on sorter selection.</p>
<h3>Pop-Up Wheel Diverter</h3>
<p>Pop-up wheel (or pop-up belt) diverters are embedded in a roller conveyor surface. When activated, wheels or a narrow belt pop up above the roller surface and divert the item at a right angle. Pop-up diverters suit lower-speed applications with fewer destinations. Pivoting wheel sorters are a related, faster design: Hytrol <a href="https://blog.hytrol.com/hytrol-sc-pivoting-wheel-sorter/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">states its ProSort SC</a> can run at up to 300 FPM. They are simpler and less expensive than sliding shoe or crossbelt sorters. See <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer conveyors</a> for detailed coverage of right-angle divert technology.</p>
<h3>Tilt-Tray Sorter</h3>
<p>Tilt-tray sorters use individual tray carriers that tilt to one side at the sort point, dropping the item into the chute. The tray resets to level for the next load cycle. Tilt-tray sorters handle small, flat items well (apparel, books, small electronics) but struggle with round or irregularly shaped items that roll off the tray prematurely.</p>
