<h2 id="accum-heading">Accumulation &amp; Merge Conveyors</h2>
<h3>Zero-Pressure Accumulation</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Zero-pressure accumulation conveyors</a> stage products without contact between items. Each zone holds one product and stops independently. When a downstream zone clears, the upstream zone releases its product. This prevents product damage, label scuffing, and case crushing that occurs when products press against each other under back-pressure.</p>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR (motor-driven roller) conveyors</a> are the standard zero-pressure accumulation technology in modern distribution centers. Each zone contains a motorized roller that drives the other rollers in the zone through O-ring or poly-V belts. Photoeye sensors detect product presence and control zone release. MDR zones run only when product is present, which cuts energy use compared with conveyors that run continuously.</p>
<h3>Merge Conveyors</h3>
<p>Merge conveyors combine flows from multiple pick zones, pack stations, or parallel conveyors onto a single outbound line. The merge must insert items into gaps on the main line without collision or stoppage. Merge technologies:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Speed-up merge:</strong> The merging conveyor accelerates to match the main line speed and inserts items into natural gaps. Simplest and lowest cost. Works when main line has sufficient gap density.</li>
<li><strong>Slug merge:</strong> Holds multiple items on the merge conveyor, then releases them as a group when a gap opens on the main line. Higher throughput than one-at-a-time merging.</li>
<li><strong>Servo-controlled merge:</strong> Servo drives precisely time individual item release to insert into specific gaps on the main line. Highest throughput and most reliable but most expensive.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="asrs-heading">AS/RS &amp; AMR Integration</h2>
<p>Automated Storage and Retrieval Systems (AS/RS) and Autonomous Mobile Robots (AMRs) connect to the conveyor network through transfer points, staging conveyors, and accumulation zones. The conveyor system is the fixed backbone, AS/RS and AMR systems are the flexible storage and transport layer.</p>
<h3>AS/RS Conveyor Interface</h3>
<p>Conveyors connect to AS/RS cranes and shuttles at the end-of-aisle transfer point. Infeed conveyors deliver loads (cases, totes, pallets) to the AS/RS for storage. Outfeed conveyors receive retrieved loads for picking or shipping. Accumulation zones buffer loads between the conveyor's continuous flow and the AS/RS crane's cycle-based operation. Sizing the <a href="/conveyor-types/buffer-conveyors/">accumulation buffer</a> correctly prevents bottlenecks, the buffer must absorb the AS/RS cycle time variation without starving downstream operations.</p>
<p>When the conveyor and AS/RS come from different suppliers, the interface specification must define load dimensions, transfer height, conveyor speed, handshake signals, and communication protocol (commonly <a href="https://www.odva.org/technology-standards/key-technologies/ethernet-ip/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EtherNet/IP</a> or PROFINET).</p>
<h3>AMR Conveyor Interface</h3>
<p>AMR integration uses conveyor-mounted load/unload stations where AMRs pick up or deliver totes and cases. The station includes a short conveyor section, presence sensors, and a communication interface (Wi-Fi, 5G, or infrared) that coordinates the AMR docking cycle with conveyor motion. AMR fleets typically integrate with standard conveyor systems through WCS (Warehouse Control System) software, so define which system owns the handshake before purchase.</p>
<h2 id="elevation-heading">Vertical Transport in Distribution</h2>
<p>Multi-level distribution centers and mezzanine operations need vertical conveyors to move cases, totes, and pallets between levels. Three technologies serve this need:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Spiral conveyors:</strong> Continuous-flow spirals move cases and totes through elevation changes without stopping in a compact footprint. Cost is driven by height and load per turn. See also: <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/cooling/">spiral cooling conveyors</a> for food applications.</li>
<li><strong><a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">Vertical reciprocating conveyors (VRCs)</a>:</strong> Platform-based lifts that carry cases or pallets between levels. Cycle-based (not continuous). Suited for lower-throughput, heavy-load applications.</li>
<li><strong>Incline/decline belt conveyors:</strong> Simple and low cost for moderate elevation changes, but they need the most floor length. The usable angle depends on the product and the belt surface, so confirm it with product tests.</li>
</ul>
<p>For layout and cost optimization, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> and <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated systems guide</a>.</p>
