<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What conveyors are used in warehouses?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Belt conveyors, <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a>, sortation (shoe, crossbelt, tilt-tray), <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a>, merge, and vertical conveyors. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> are standard for modern zero-pressure accumulation.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is a sortation conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A sortation conveyor automatically diverts products to designated lanes based on barcode or RFID data. Types include sliding shoe (gentle, high-speed), crossbelt (widest product range), pop-up wheel (lower-speed), and tilt-tray (small items). Rated speeds vary by technology and model.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is AS/RS conveyor integration?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Conveyors connect AS/RS (Automated Storage/Retrieval Systems) to picking and shipping. Infeed conveyors deliver loads for storage; outfeed conveyors receive retrieved loads. Accumulation zones buffer between continuous conveyor flow and AS/RS cycle timing.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How do merge conveyors work?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Merge conveyors combine multiple streams into one line using speed-up merges (accelerate into gaps), slug merges (release groups), or servo-controlled merges (precise individual timing). Servo merges provide highest throughput and reliability.</p>

</div>
